\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the 402{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration by Equation\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:accel}, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz to 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation at the equator (0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and orbit (0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is also the median; it runs to 4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the 5{} TRAPPIST--1{} windows. For the most demanding case, transiting TRAPPIST-1\nobreakspace  {}b, 29{} per cent of the orbital phase lies above the default ceiling and 1{} per cent above the widened one, which falls 0.01{} per cent short of that planet's own maximum acceleration.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{3}{6}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration by Equation~\ref {eq:accel}, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz to \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation at the equator (\AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and orbit (\AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is also the median; it runs to \AccelCeilMax \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the \AccelNWide {} \AccelWideStar {} windows. For the most demanding case, transiting TRAPPIST-1~b, \TrapPhaseHi {} per cent of the orbital phase lies above the default ceiling and \TrapPhaseLo {} per cent above the widened one, which falls \AccelShortPctMax {} per cent short of that planet's own maximum acceleration}{figure.3}{}}
