\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Can two crossings be one carrier?} Every pair of crossings toward one star, in two execution blocks, within 833\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of each other in the star's frame; stars are identified by position, so the two archival-tail crossings of HD\nobreakspace  {}14055{} pair with its catalogued ones. $\Delta \nu $ is the stellar-frame offset and $\dot  \nu _1$, $\dot  \nu _2$ the drifts fitted independently in the two blocks. A transmitter at constant line-of-sight acceleration would put the pair at $\Delta \nu =\frac  {1}{2}(\dot  \nu _1+\dot  \nu _2)\Delta t$; the last column gives how far the pair is from that, in units of the drift grid's own resolution carried over $\Delta t$. Italic entries are pairs for which that resolution exceeds the window searched, so the comparison has no power and only the agreement of the pairs' number with chance bears on them. The last row is the control: the only crossings in this survey that are present again, which the same test accepts.}}{12}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pairs}{{4}{12}{\textbf {Can two crossings be one carrier?} Every pair of crossings toward one star, in two execution blocks, within \WdHalfKms \,km\,s$^{-1}$ of each other in the star's frame; stars are identified by position, so the two archival-tail crossings of \TjDeepStar {} pair with its catalogued ones. $\Delta \nu $ is the stellar-frame offset and $\dot \nu _1$, $\dot \nu _2$ the drifts fitted independently in the two blocks. A transmitter at constant line-of-sight acceleration would put the pair at $\Delta \nu =\frac {1}{2}(\dot \nu _1+\dot \nu _2)\Delta t$; the last column gives how far the pair is from that, in units of the drift grid's own resolution carried over $\Delta t$. Italic entries are pairs for which that resolution exceeds the window searched, so the comparison has no power and only the agreement of the pairs' number with chance bears on them. The last row is the control: the only crossings in this survey that are present again, which the same test accepts}{table.4}{}}
