\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Why this survey reports no confirmed signal although it produced an interesting event.} \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}The dynamic spectrum at 61 Vir's position around the crossing at 346.313811\,GHz, observed 2017 May 20, with the fitted drift of +3182\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ as the dashed track; the 225{} integrations are binned into 9{} and plotted in units of each bin's noise. At 119$\pm $20\,mJy against 303\,mJy per integration the carrier is 0.39{} standard deviations in any one of them: it exists only in the coherent stack of all 38{} minutes on source. \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}The same rows shifted by that drift, which carries the carrier 22{} channels of 488.3\,kHz across the 57{} minutes the observation spans; 8{} of the 9{} bins are positive at the marked channel. \emph  {(c)}\nobreakspace  {}The de-drifted stack at the star (black) and at the 8{} control positions with retained dynamic spectra (grey), in units of its own noise; dashed, the trigger. \emph  {(d)}\nobreakspace  {}The same statistic at each of the 512{} rank-screen control positions: the star (solid) against the highest control (dotted). \emph  {(e)}\nobreakspace  {}The 3{} other blocks covering that cell in the star's rest frame, combined, with zero at the \emph  {predicted} channel, which the frame transport moves between epochs (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:cand} dates them). Dashed, the $T_{\rm  pers}=9.7$ a carrier persisting at the discovery flux would have given; circled, the $T_{\rm  rep}=+0.39$ read there at the carried drift, 4.9$\sigma $ away on Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:excl}. \emph  {(f)}\nobreakspace  {}The second event, CP$-$72 2713{} at 344.269746\,GHz: its discovery stack (black) and the same cell 2.05\,hours later in a block 1.1{} times deeper (blue), with the same two marks at $T_{\rm  pers}=6.2$ and, in that one block, $T_{\rm  rep}=-0.57$ --- not the combination over all 5{} covering blocks that Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:cand} prints. The two events imply isotropic-equivalent powers of $5.1\times 10^{14}$\,W at 8.5\,pc and $8.9\times 10^{14}$\,W at 36.7\,pc.}}{13}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:event}{{6}{13}{\textbf {Why this survey reports no confirmed signal although it produced an interesting event.} \emph {(a)}~The dynamic spectrum at \EvAStar 's position around the crossing at \EvAFreq \,GHz, observed \EvADate , with the fitted drift of \EvADrift \,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ as the dashed track; the \EvANInt {} integrations are binned into \EvANBin {} and plotted in units of each bin's noise. At \EvAFlux $\pm $\EvAFluxErr \,mJy against \EvARmsInt \,mJy per integration the carrier is \EvAPerIntSig {} standard deviations in any one of them: it exists only in the coherent stack of all \EvADwellMin {} minutes on source. \emph {(b)}~The same rows shifted by that drift, which carries the carrier \EvADriftChan {} channels of \EvAChanwkHz \,kHz across the \EvAElapsedMin {} minutes the observation spans; \EvANBinPos {} of the \EvANBin {} bins are positive at the marked channel. \emph {(c)}~The de-drifted stack at the star (black) and at the \RcNRetCtrl {} control positions with retained dynamic spectra (grey), in units of its own noise; dashed, the trigger. \emph {(d)}~The same statistic at each of the \NCtrl {} rank-screen control positions: the star (solid) against the highest control (dotted). \emph {(e)}~The \EvANRep {} other blocks covering that cell in the star's rest frame, combined, with zero at the \emph {predicted} channel, which the frame transport moves between epochs (Table~\ref {tab:cand} dates them). Dashed, the $T_{\rm pers}=\EvATPers $ a carrier persisting at the discovery flux would have given; circled, the $T_{\rm rep}=\EvATRep $ read there at the carried drift, \EvAExcl $\sigma $ away on Eq.~\ref {eq:excl}. \emph {(f)}~The second event, \EvBStar {} at \EvBFreq \,GHz: its discovery stack (black) and the same cell \EvBDeepH \,hours later in a block \EvBDeepRatio {} times deeper (blue), with the same two marks at $T_{\rm pers}=\EvBDeepTPers $ and, in that one block, $T_{\rm rep}=\EvBDeepTObs $ --- not the combination over all \ZdCpNRep {} covering blocks that Table~\ref {tab:cand} prints. The two events imply isotropic-equivalent powers of $\EvAEirp \times 10^{\EvAEirpExp }$\,W at \EvADistPc \,pc and $\EvBEirp \times 10^{\EvBEirpExp }$\,W at \EvBDistPc \,pc}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{sec:stack}{{5.7}{13}{A stellar-frame multi-epoch stacked search}{subsection.5.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The two events of Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:event}}, the crossings whose stellar statistic exceeds all 512{} control positions of their own window. Repeat epochs measured are the blocks at which the predicted stellar-frame cell was read; the indented rows give how many of those the released catalogue carries and how many blocks it lists as covering the frequency at all, which differ because the archival extension is deposited separately. $T_{\rm  rep}$ combines every repeat epoch by inverse variance at the carried drift: it is one cell and one trial, so it may be negative, and it is the quantity $T_{\rm  pers}$ is compared against. The indented readings beneath it are the largest single epoch at that drift and the largest over every drift trial, each a maximum and so positive on average whatever is there; the single-epoch reading in the deepest block is the one Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:event}(f) marks, and it is not the combination. The exclusion is Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:excl}, and beneath it the fraction of the discovery amplitude $S$ still ruled out at the trigger, $(T_{\rm  rep}+5)/T_{\rm  pers}$, and the chance $\Phi (T_{\rm  pers}-5)$ that a carrier persisting at $S$, and then at the trigger amplitude $5S/T_\star $, would have satisfied the criterion at all.}}{14}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cand}{{5}{14}{\textbf {The two events of Fig.~\ref {fig:event}}, the crossings whose stellar statistic exceeds all \NCtrl {} control positions of their own window. Repeat epochs measured are the blocks at which the predicted stellar-frame cell was read; the indented rows give how many of those the released catalogue carries and how many blocks it lists as covering the frequency at all, which differ because the archival extension is deposited separately. $T_{\rm rep}$ combines every repeat epoch by inverse variance at the carried drift: it is one cell and one trial, so it may be negative, and it is the quantity $T_{\rm pers}$ is compared against. The indented readings beneath it are the largest single epoch at that drift and the largest over every drift trial, each a maximum and so positive on average whatever is there; the single-epoch reading in the deepest block is the one Fig.~\ref {fig:event}(f) marks, and it is not the combination. The exclusion is Eq.~\ref {eq:excl}, and beneath it the fraction of the discovery amplitude $S$ still ruled out at the trigger, $(T_{\rm rep}+5)/T_{\rm pers}$, and the chance $\Phi (T_{\rm pers}-5)$ that a carrier persisting at $S$, and then at the trigger amplitude $5S/T_\star $, would have satisfied the criterion at all}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The detection logic, with the count at each level. Three levels and no more: a \emph  {crossing} is an event requiring investigation, an \emph  {unattributed crossing} is a target for follow-up, and a \emph  {confirmed signal} would be a detection. Coincidence with a catalogued transition is a flag and not a disposal, so all 56{} crossings continue past it; the spatial rank and the visibility fit are diagnostics attached to the middle level and decide nothing. The reserved hold-out is drawn as a separate branch because a transmitter in it would matter whatever its calibration role. Every count is read from the ledger, which lists every crossing the search produced, including those in the archival-tail blocks and the quality-failing block that no expectation is computed over, so the figure, Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:ledger} and the text cannot disagree.}}{14}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:chain}{{7}{14}{The detection logic, with the count at each level. Three levels and no more: a \emph {crossing} is an event requiring investigation, an \emph {unattributed crossing} is a target for follow-up, and a \emph {confirmed signal} would be a detection. Coincidence with a catalogued transition is a flag and not a disposal, so all \CnCross {} crossings continue past it; the spatial rank and the visibility fit are diagnostics attached to the middle level and decide nothing. The reserved hold-out is drawn as a separate branch because a transmitter in it would matter whatever its calibration role. Every count is read from the ledger, which lists every crossing the search produced, including those in the archival-tail blocks and the quality-failing block that no expectation is computed over, so the figure, Table~\ref {tab:ledger} and the text cannot disagree}{figure.7}{}}
